        "contact": {
          "url": "https://noteapiconnector.com/jmespath-free-api"
        },
        "description": "The JMESPath free API provides a serverless endpoint for running JSON transformations using the JMESPath query language. It enables users to quickly filter, reshape, and extract data from JSON without the need for deploying any infrastructure. This API is ideal for prototyping and low-volume automation tasks.",
        "servers": [
          {
            "description": "Production serverless endpoint",
            "url": "https://noteapiconnector-tools.vercel.app/api"
          }
        ],
        "termsOfService": "https://noteapiconnector.com/terms-of-service",
        "title": "JMESPath Free API",
        "version": "1.0.0",
        "tagline": "Run JMESPath queries against arbitrary JSON over a serverless HTTP endpoint",
        "homepage": "https://noteapiconnector.com",
        "docs_url": "https://freepublicapis.com/jmespath-free-api",
        "about_md": "[JMESPath Free API](https://freepublicapis.com/jmespath-free-api) is a serverless endpoint that runs [JMESPath](https://jmespath.org) queries against arbitrary JSON payloads. It is hosted on Vercel and operated by [Note API Connector](https://noteapiconnector.com), where JMESPath transformations are also offered as part of their wider Notion integration tooling.\n\nWhat you get from the API:\n\n- A single `POST /jmespath` endpoint that accepts a JSON body containing `data` (the JSON to query) and `query` (the JMESPath expression).\n- The result of evaluating the JMESPath expression against the supplied data, returned as JSON.\n- A way to filter, reshape, and extract values from JSON without standing up your own JMESPath runtime.\n\nThe service is intended for prototyping and low-volume automation rather than heavy production traffic. CORS is enabled, and the public catalogue listing does not mention authentication keys or hard rate limits.",
        "entity_desc": {
          "jmes_path": "Evaluation of a JMESPath expression against a supplied JSON document via `POST /jmespath` with `data` and `query` fields in the JSON body."
        },
